5	According to the 2014 dissertation *"Cardiovascular Disease Risk Factors Among Emerging Adults in College"* by Demetrius Alexander Abshire, **waist circumference (WC)** was found to be the best predictor of systolic blood pressure among emerging adults in college.\n\nSupporting evidence from this dissertation includes:\n1. The Chapter 4 conclusions state: "Waist circumference was a marginally better predictor of SBP in college students compared to SAD and BMI. Waist circumference was also a predictor of blood pressure independent of BMI. These findings suggest that the easily obtainable WC, as a simple anthropometric measure of abdominal obesity, could be used in evaluating hypertension risk among college students."\n2. Table 4.2 (Predictors of Systolic Blood Pressure) shows the model including WC had the highest Step 2 Adjusted R² (0.424) for systolic blood pressure, compared to 0.379 for the BMI-only model and 0.378 for the SAD-only model, meaning WC explained more variance in systolic blood pressure than the other obesity measures.\n3. Table 4.4 (Predictive Ability of Abdominal Obesity Controlling for Body Mass Index) demonstrates that when controlling for BMI, WC was a statistically significant independent predictor of systolic blood pressure (standardized β = 0.777, p<0.01), while SAD was not a significant predictor (β = 0.256, p>0.05) in the same models.\n4. The dissertation's Chapter 5 summary of findings explicitly notes: "Although all measures of general and abdominal obesity predicted blood pressure, waist circumference was the strongest predictor explaining the most variance in systolic blood pressure."\n5. An earlier section of the dissertation also confirms: "Findings suggest that waist circumference was the best predictor of systolic blood pressure among emerging adults in college."	фс	2026-05-04 17:59:51.37446	tencent/hy3-preview:free	2026-05-04 18:00:20.534472	CORRECTED	According to this dissertation, which obesity measure was found to be the best 