9	All information below is from the 2014 scientific paper *Cardiovascular Disease Risk Factors Among Emerging Adults in College* by Demetrius Alexander Abshire:\n\n### Environmental and situational barriers\n1.  **College food environment**: While healthy food options are available on campus, they are overshadowed by an abundance of unhealthy options. Fixed-price buffet dining makes healthy and unhealthy options equally costly, so taste preferences and greater access to unhealthy foods are stronger drivers of poor dietary choices than cost or lack of healthy options. Buffet-style dining specifically is linked to overconsumption of unhealthy foods.\n2.  **Time constraints, convenience, and social influences**: These are cited as key barriers to choosing healthy foods while in college.\n3.  **Built environment barriers for rural Appalachian students**: Residents of rural Eastern Appalachian Kentucky face built environment barriers to healthy eating and physical activity. The paper references quantitative data finding greater access to fast food restaurants is associated with higher fast food consumption among young adults with lower incomes in this region.\n\n### Perceptual and behavioral barriers\n1.  **Lack of immediate health threat perception**: Students emphasized that the absence of an immediate, perceived health threat is a major barrier to behavior change; they reported they would not alter unhealthy behaviors unless facing an immediate health issue. This aligns with findings that college men are aware of health needs but only take action when experiencing extreme physical or emotional pain. Many college students also define health as the absence of disease, reducing motivation to engage in preventive behaviors like physical activity.\n2.  **Perception that unhealthy behavior is acceptable in college**: Students perceive it is acceptable to engage in unhealthy behaviors during their college years, which acts as a barrier to adopting cardiovascular disease (CVD)-preventive behaviors.\n3.  **Perceived invincibility**: The paper cites prior research finding that perceived invincibility to illness is a barrier to healthy eating and exercise among emerging adults, which may explain why students ignore long-term CVD risk even when aware of risk factors.\n4.  **Physical activity preferences**: While students recognized aerobic activity is more important for heart health, most reported neglecting aerobic exercise in favor of weight lifting, reducing the cardiovascular benefits of their physical activity.\n\n### Gender-specific barriers for male college students\nParticipants perceived that men’s lifestyles increase CVD risk: men are less likely to prepare their own meals, less conscious of food choices, and less likely to engage in aerobic activity, with higher fast food consumption than women. Social perceptions that women pay more attention to health and have lower CVD risk also contribute to gendered disparities in healthy behavior engagement.	Environmental and situational barriers include the college food environment, where healthy options exist but are overshadowed by an abundance of unhealthy foods, with buffet-style dining contributing to overeating and poor dietary choices. Time constraints, convenience, and social influences also act as key barriers to engaging in healthy behaviors.\r\n\r\nPerceptual and behavioral barriers include a lack of immediate health threat perception, meaning students are less likely to change behavior unless they perceive an immediate risk, and the belief that unhealthy behavior is acceptable during college years, which reduces motivation for preventive actions. Additionally, although students recognize the importance of aerobic activity, they often neglect it in practice.	2026-05-04 18:31:11.754649	tencent/hy3-preview:free	2026-05-04 18:34:36.053837	CORRECTED	What barriers were identified that prevent college students from engaging in healthy lifestyle behaviors?	\N
12	According to "The Heart and Pulmonary Circulation at High Altitudes" by Dante Penaloza, Javier Arias-Stella (2007), the primary physiological change in the pulmonary vasculature responsible for pulmonary hypertension in healthy highlanders is **delayed postnatal remodeling of the distal pulmonary arterial branches**, which results in a persistent fetal-like histological pattern: an increased amount of smooth muscle cells (SMCs) in the distal pulmonary arteries and muscularization of pulmonary arterioles. This structural change increases pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR), the direct driver of pulmonary hypertension in this population.\n\nThe paper notes that vasoconstriction is only a secondary contributing factor, as administration of oxygen reduces pulmonary artery pressure by just 15–20% in healthy highlanders. The structural nature of the primary change is confirmed by the slow reversal of pulmonary hypertension: pulmonary artery pressure only normalizes after approximately 2 years of residence at sea level, as the delayed vascular remodeling gradually resolves. In contrast, infants born at sea level undergo prompt postnatal remodeling of distal pulmonary arteries, with rapid loss of excess SMCs and no muscularization of arterioles, resulting in normal pulmonary artery pressure in adulthood.	\N	2026-05-04 23:05:56.370587	tencent/hy3-preview:free	2026-05-04 23:07:33.948778	APPROVED	What physiological change in the pulmonary vasculature is primarily responsible for pulmonary hypertension in healthy highlanders?	\N
13	The information comes from *"The Heart and Pulmonary Circulation at High Altitudes"* by Dante Penaloza, Javier Arias-Stella (2007).\n\nThe primary physiological change in the pulmonary vasculature responsible for pulmonary hypertension (PH) in healthy highlanders is **delayed postnatal remodeling of the distal pulmonary arterial branches**, which leads to a persistent increased amount of smooth muscle cells (SMCs) in the distal pulmonary arteries and arterioles, increasing pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR).\n\nAs described in the paper:\n- The abstract states: "Pulmonary hypertension in healthy highlanders is related to a delayed postnatal remodeling of the distal pulmonary arterial branches."\n- Histological comparisons between high-altitude (HA) natives and sea-level (SL) residents show that while both groups have a fetal pattern of increased SMCs in small pulmonary arteries and muscularized arterioles at birth, SL residents rapidly lose this SMC prominence after birth, with thin vascular walls and widened lumens by adulthood. In contrast, HA natives retain this fetal pattern of increased SMCs in the distal pulmonary vasculature throughout life, reflecting delayed remodeling.\n- The paper explicitly confirms: "The evidence indicates that the main factor responsible for PH in healthy highlanders is the increased amount of SMCs in the distal pulmonary arteries and arterioles, which increases the PVR. Vasoconstriction is a secondary factor because the administration of oxygen decreases the PAP only by 15% to 20%. Hypervolemia, polycythemia, and increased blood viscosity, although considered causal factors in earlier studies, are now considered secondary factors."	\N	2026-05-04 23:21:19.565524	tencent/hy3-preview:free	2026-05-04 23:22:00.248521	APPROVED	What physiological change in the pulmonary vasculature is primarily responsible for pulmonary hypertension in healthy highlanders?	\N
